A corner is any two axes, and only one vertex is not a corner

PR #542 said it would make D×D, D×V and D×H corners all work. It shipped only
D×D. Mid-build I found a real pathology — a point 11 units down a PERFECT
horizontal had both of that target's diagonals in tolerance, cornered them, and
collapsed onto the target, throwing a zero-error snap away to travel 11 units —
and I fixed it by banning 45° pairings. That was the wrong cut. The pathology is
about two axes coming from the SAME target, which cross AT that target; the
angle between them has nothing to do with it. Banning the angle took the
octolinear corner with it, and a vertical edge meeting a diagonal at 135° is the
commonest corner this app draws.

So the cut moves to where the constraints come from. Any two non-parallel
families corner, mixed pairs included. Two axes through the same target are not
a corner at all — the lock is "snap onto this vertex", and it holds only within
tolerance of the vertex by plain distance, which is the honest test of being
near it. That kills the 11-unit collapse exactly (distance 11 > tolerance 10)
while leaving every genuine crossing alone, and it subsumes the alignment-based
guard it replaces.

Which two families pair is still decided by family and never by ranking them —
straight pair, then diagonals, then the one mixed pair a lone straight and a
lone diagonal leave. A ranking would re-choose the pair as the pointer moved,
which is the per-family tie-break's flutter one level up.

A 135° corner can displace up to 2.6× the tolerance where a right angle caps at
√2×. That is inherent to squaring a corner that is not square, and it is not
capped.

Three existing expectations move, all of them encoding the rule being replaced.
Two asserted that a 45° pair does not corner — one for point targets, one for a
straight guide crossing a diagonal one — and both now assert the corner. The
third, the polygon vertex-drag test, began cornering incidentally when the apex
caught a 45° line from the far base corner; its drop point moves 10 units so it
keeps testing drag plumbing rather than corner arbitration, which belongs to
polygonSnap's own tests.

